#: ../../source/docs/apmcopter-programming-attitude-control-2.rst:5
#: 003cf1332b244750a71f5d3997dc2cdf
msgid "Copter Attitude Control"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/apmcopter-programming-attitude-control-2.rst:7
#: f27cf4131d4d4baf9d9cd6af7099ac1a
msgid "Below is a high level diagram showing how the attitude control is done."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/apmcopter-programming-attitude-control-2.rst:12
#: cbdf04bc85c7474ea97361e232f7eff8
msgid "Below describes what is done for each axis.  A P controller converts the angle error (the difference between the target angle and actual angle) into a desired rotation rate followed by a PID controller to convert the rotate rate error into a high level motor command. The \"square root controller\" portion of the diagram shows the curve used with the angle control's P controller."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/apmcopter-programming-attitude-control-2.rst:18
#: 19689e89d3414924b62f46069946d020
msgid "The diagram below shows the code path followed from pilot input down to pwm output."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/apmcopter-programming-attitude-control-2.rst:23
#: 7d1ad1eb460a4c03bc2630b93a8d5f6e
msgid "On every update (i.e. 400hz on Pixhawk, 100hz on APM2.x) the following happens:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/apmcopter-programming-attitude-control-2.rst:26
#: 445a0f29bcad4c10bc0ccb1b53526e5b
msgid "the top level flight-mode.cpp's \"update_flight_mode()\" function is called.  This function checks the vehicle's flight mode (ie. \"control_mode\" variable) and then calls the appropriate <flight mode>_run() function (i.e. `stabilize_run <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/blob/master/ArduCopter/mode_stabilize.cpp#L9>`__ for stabilize mode, `rtl_run <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/blob/master/ArduCopter/mode_rtl.cpp#L49>`__ for RTL mode, etc).  The <flight mode>_run() function can be found in the appropriately named mode_<flight mode>.cpp file (i.e. `mode_stabilize.cpp <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/blob/master/ArduCopter/mode_stabilize.cpp>`__, `mode_rtl.cpp <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/blob/master/ArduCopter/mode_rtl.cpp>`__, etc)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/apmcopter-programming-attitude-control-2.rst:37
#: d9aa95d99d674c14a80a39b7ceaa4d46
msgid "the <flight mode>_run function is responsible for converting the user's input (found in g.rc_1.control_in, g.rc_2.control_in, etc) into a lean angle, rotation rate, climb rate, etc that is appropriate for this flight mode.  For example `AltHold <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/blob/master/ArduCopter/mode_althold.cpp#L22>`__ converts the user's roll and pitch input into lean angles (in degrees), the yaw input is converted into a rotation rate (in degrees per second) and the throttle input is converted to a climb rate (in cm/s)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/apmcopter-programming-attitude-control-2.rst:46
#: e5929b4997f747f09f2b77341051d4ac
msgid "the last thing the <flight mode>_run function must do is pass these desired angles, rates etc into Attitude Control and/or Position Control libraries (these are both held in the `AC_AttitudeControl <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/tree/master/libraries/AC_AttitudeControl>`__ folder)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/apmcopter-programming-attitude-control-2.rst:51
#: 529d728b89404d0d85e7d2fa153a8720
msgid "The `AC_AttitudeControl <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/blob/master/libraries/AC_AttitudeControl/AC_AttitudeControl.h>`__ library provides 5 possible ways to control the attitude of the vehicle, the most common 3 are described below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/apmcopter-programming-attitude-control-2.rst:55
#: 1c3b62e971424baea3ece6456bec87e3
msgid "`angle_ef_roll_pitch_rate_ef_yaw() <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/blob/master/libraries/AC_AttitudeControl/AC_AttitudeControl.h#L98>`__ : this accepts an \"earth frame\" angle for roll and pitch, and an \"earth frame\" rate for yaw.  For example providing this function roll = -1000, pitch = -1500, yaw = 500 means lean the vehicle left to 10degrees, pitch forward to 15degrees and rotate right at 5deg/second."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/apmcopter-programming-attitude-control-2.rst:61
#: 967df2eee3bf4c38b97b9dee65d176ca
msgid "`angle_ef_roll_pitch_yaw() <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/blob/master/libraries/AC_AttitudeControl/AC_AttitudeControl.h#L102>`__ : this accepts \"earth frame\" angles for roll, pitch and yaw. similar to above except providing yaw of 500 means rotate the vehicle to 5 degrees east of north."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/apmcopter-programming-attitude-control-2.rst:65
#: 650563493bd84844bac9191c921d4b02
msgid "`rate_bf_roll_pitch_yaw() <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/blob/master/libraries/AC_AttitudeControl/AC_AttitudeControl.h#L108>`__ : this accepts a \"body frame\" rate (in degrees/sec) for roll pitch and yaw.  For example providing this function roll = -1000, pitch = -1500, yaw = 500 would lead to the vehicle rolling left at 10deg/sec, pitching forward at 15deg/sec and rotating about the vehicle's z axis at 5 deg/sec."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/apmcopter-programming-attitude-control-2.rst:72
#: 0bef8e704902446f8fdf386c5274124e
msgid "After any calls to these functions are made the `AC_AttitudeControl::rate_controller_run() <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/blob/master/libraries/AC_AttitudeControl/AC_AttitudeControl.h#L114>`__ is called.  This converts the output from the methods listed above into roll, pitch and yaw inputs which are sent to the `AP_Motors <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/tree/master/libraries/AP_Motors>`__ library via it's `set_roll, set_pitch, set_yaw and set_throttle <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/blob/master/libraries/AP_Motors/AP_Motors_Class.h#L99>`__ methods."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/apmcopter-programming-attitude-control-2.rst:80
#: 13d075f52b6e4e0a979844d54a9c07e3
msgid "The `AC_PosControl <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/blob/master/libraries/AC_AttitudeControl/AC_PosControl.h>`__ library allows 3D position control of the vehicle.  Normally only the simpler Z-axis (i.e. altitude control) methods are used because more complicated 3D position flight modes (i.e. `Loiter <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/blob/master/ArduCopter/control_loiter.cpp#L30>`__) make use of the `AC_WPNav <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/blob/master/libraries/AC_WPNav/AC_WPNav.h>`__ library.  In any case, some commonly used methods of this library include:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/apmcopter-programming-attitude-control-2.rst:90
#: 8844eb6cbe114946aee3f44d6b69bc33
msgid "`set_alt_target_from_climb_rate() <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/blob/master/libraries/AC_AttitudeControl/AC_PosControl.h#L109>`__ : this accepts a climb rate in cm/s and updates an absolute altitude target"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/apmcopter-programming-attitude-control-2.rst:93
#: 8b8b8171c66643dfa24c0f9dbb8fdd02
msgid "`set_pos_target() <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/blob/master/libraries/AC_AttitudeControl/AC_PosControl.h#L171>`__ : this accepts a 3D position vector which is an offset from home in cm"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/apmcopter-programming-attitude-control-2.rst:97
#: 5ba60fceed1640d1b7759d139034e5bf
msgid "If any methods in AC_PosControl are called then the flight mode code must also call the `AC_PosControl::update_z\\_controller() <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/blob/master/libraries/AC_AttitudeControl/AC_PosControl.h#L134>`__ method.  This will run the z-axis position control PID loops and send low-level throttle level to the `AP_Motors <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/tree/master/libraries/AP_Motors>`__ library.  If any xy-axis methods are called then `AC_PosControl::update_xy_controller() <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/blob/master/libraries/AC_AttitudeControl/AC_PosControl.h#L202>`__ must be called."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/apmcopter-programming-attitude-control-2.rst:107
#: b38afad46afa4a4da288d412473f9cdf
msgid "The AP_Motors library holds the \"motor mixing\" code.  This code is responsible for converting the roll, pitch, yaw and throttle values received from the AC_AttitudeControl and AC_PosControl libraries into absolute motor outputs (i.e. PWM values).  So the higher level libs would make use of these functions:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/apmcopter-programming-attitude-control-2.rst:113
#: 13510b63d97d4adcaac563a7c89ff805
msgid "`set_roll(), set_pitch(), set_yaw() <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/blob/master/libraries/AP_Motors/AP_Motors_Class.h#L99>`__ : accepts roll, pitch and yaw values in the range of -4500 ~ 4500.  These are not desired angles or even rates but rather just a value.  For example set_roll(-4500) would mean roll left as fast as possible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/apmcopter-programming-attitude-control-2.rst:118
#: 61201adba8c04deab968bce71424663c
msgid "set_throttle() : accepts an absolute throttle value in the range of 0 ~ 1000.  0 = motors off, 1000 = full throttle."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/apmcopter-programming-attitude-control-2.rst:121
#: 7382e1931c064ea0816122dd0efd555e
msgid "There are different classes for each frame type (quad, Y6, traditional helicopter) but in each there is an \"`output_armed <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/blob/master/libraries/AP_Motors/AP_MotorsMatrix.cpp#L123>`__\\ \" function which is responsible for implementing the conversion of these roll, pitch, yaw and throttle values into pwm outputs.  This conversion often includes implementing a \"stability patch\" which handles prioritising one axis of control over another when the input requests are outside the physical limits of the frame (i.e. max throttle and max roll is not possible with a quad because some motors must be less than others to cause a roll).  At the bottom of the \"output_armed\" function there is a call to the hal.rcout->write() which passes the desired pwm values to the AP_HAL layer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/apmcopter-programming-attitude-control-2.rst:123
#: cdcad6d3bd2e4306b69d522e73185f1e
msgid "The `AP_HAL <https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/tree/master/libraries/AP_HAL>`__ libraries (hardware abstraction layer) provides a consistent interface for all boards.  In particular the hal.rc_out_write() function will cause the specified PWM received from the AP_Motors class to appear on the appropriate pwm pin out for the board."
msgstr ""

